  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1225 AM CDT Fri May 8 2026\n\nThe upper low (from northern Mexico) will weaken and open into a \ntrough as it progresses east across Texas on Saturday. The warming \ntrend will continue Saturday with highs ranging mostly from the mid \n80s southeast to mid 90s in the western Big Country. As a ridge \naloft builds over the southwestern CONUS, a shortwave trough will \ndrop southeast across portions of the Red River Valley and West \nTexas Sunday afternoon. A cold front will move south across our \nnorthwestern counties Sunday morning, reaching our far southeastern \ncounties by 7 PM. Depending on the specifics with timing of the \nfront, temperatures could be considerably cooler than what is \ncurrently forecast across the Big Country. The arrival of the front \nand upper level shortwave trough will bring a chance of showers and \nthunderstorms to a good portion of our area Sunday afternoon, with \nlingering chances in our southeastern counties through early Sunday \nnight. With at least moderate instability and effective bulk shear \nincreasing to 35-40 knots, a few strong to severe storms will be \npossible. \n\nAfter the frontal passage, temperatures will be briefly cooler \nMonday with a surface high pressure ridge extending from the Midwest \nsouthwest into Texas. Dry and hotter conditions to follow Tuesday \nthrough Thursday as an upper level ridge gradually shifts east over \nTexas. This will be accompanied by mostly clear skies and a \nstrengthened low-level thermal ridge. Highs Tuesday are expected to \nrange from the mid/upper 80s in the southeastern part of our area, \nto the mid to upper 90s in the northwestern Big Country. Highs \nWednesday and Thursday are forecast to reach the mid to upper 90s in \nmuch of the Big Country and some of the Concho Valley. Some \nlocations in the northwest Big Country could reach or slightly \nexceed the century mark, where rainfall in recent weeks has been \nlower than in areas farther east and the soil is drier.",
